Tests use Path(__file__).parent.parent for PROJECT_ROOT, read .md files directly\n- Keep PA documents under 250 lines with compact markdown tables to avoid diff-guard limits\n- WIM modality reference tables use: Modality | Frequency | Intensity | Duration | KP Ref columns\n- Bilingual format: English term (*Bahasa Malaysia term*) inline\n\n---\n\n# Ralph Progress Log - Thu, Apr 16, 2026  2:45:02 PM\nStarted autonomous agent loop for PRD completion\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-001 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story is a technical writing task, not a Frappe/Python coding task. The agent's work will be focused on updating content within Markdown files.\n\n**Technical Brief for Agent**\n\n*   **Objective:** Enhance the technical accuracy and depth of High Voltage (HV) safety procedures in BEV Information Sheets.\n*   **Files to Modify:**\n    *   `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C01/KP-01.md`\n    *   `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C01/KP-02.md`\n    *   `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C01/KP-03.md`\n*   **Frappe/Python Involvement:** None. The story does not pertain to Python code, Frappe DocTypes, or hooks. The task is purely content and documentation based.\n*   **Implementation Approach:**\n    1.  **Research Regulations:** Review content to ensure it aligns with Malaysia's OSHA 1994 and EQA 1974, plus specific BEV manufacturer standards.\n    2.  **Verify PPE:** Update Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) details to be precise for BEV Level 3 work, specifically mentioning Class 0 gloves and CAT-III multimeter requirements.\n    3.  **Add Bilingual Terms:** For key technical concepts, ensure both English (EN) and Bahasa Malaysia (BM) terms are present.\n    4.  **Cite Sources:** Add references or footnotes to link claims to the correct regulations.\n*   **Gotchas & Edge Cases:**\n    *   **Diff Size Limit:** The previous attempt failed for creating too large of a diff. Apply changes incrementally and avoid reformatting entire documents to stay under the 500-line change limit.\n    *   **Regulatory Precision:** Ensure citations are to the specific, correct sections of the referenced Malaysian laws.\n    *   **Standards Accuracy:** Double-check that the PPE specifications are current and match industry best practices for HV electrical work.\n\n\n## Story US-001 — Enhance BEV C01 KP technical depth — HV safety procedures and PPE specifications\n\n### What was implemented\n- KP-01: Updated safety glasses spec from DIN EN166 to MS ISO 16321 / ANSI Z87.1 (Malaysian standard); added Electricity Supply Act 1990 (Act 447) to references with bilingual name\n- KP-02: Added bilingual heading for Digital Multimeter section; added IEC 61010-1 / MS IEC 61010-1 standard reference to DMM spec; added OSHA 1994 (Act 514), EQA 1974 (Act 127), ESA 1990 (Act 447) to References\n- KP-03: Added OSHA 1994 and ESA 1990 citations at start of safety section; added explicit PPE table with Class 0 gloves, CAT-III multimeter, MS ISO 16321 glasses; added BM term for LOTO (Kunci-Keluar/Tag-Keluar); added Malaysian regulatory references\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- diff size anti-pattern: previous attempt generated 2183 lines by rewriting entire files. Correct approach: surgical edits only (66 lines total)\n- KP-01 was already mostly compliant; focused changes were just adding the missing Electricity Supply Act ref and updating safety glasses spec\n- CAT-III multimeter specification is a TOOL spec (not PPE), so only relevant in KP-02 (health check) and KP-03 (maintenance) — not KP-01 (workplace prep)\n\n### Gotchas\n- Windows: must use encoding=utf-8 when opening files with Python\n- Malaysian standard for safety glasses is MS ISO 16321 (not DIN EN166)\n- Act numbers: OSHA = Act 514, EQA = Act 127, ESA = Act 447, Road Transport = Act 333\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-002 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\n**Subject: Technical Brief for US-002: Enhance BEV Technical Knowledge Content**\n\n**Note:** The user story provided (US-002) is for enhancing Markdown-based technical documentation, not a Frappe/Python implementation. The following brief is adjusted accordingly.\n\n**1. Target Files:**\nThis task involves modifying Markdown (`.md`) files, not Python code. The specific files are:\n- `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KP-01.md`\n- `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KP-02.md`\n- `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KP-03.md`\n- `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KP-04.md`\n- `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KP-05.md`\n- `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KP-06.md`\n\n**2. Frappe Context:**\nThere are no Frappe DocTypes or hooks involved. The work is confined to editing the content of the specified Markdown files.\n\n**3. Implementation Approach:**\nThe agent should read, analyze, and rewrite the content within each of the six KP files. The goal is to enhance the technical theory for BEV rectification, focusing on:\n- Battery Management System (BMS) components.\n- High-Voltage (HV) cable, On-Board Charger (OBC), and battery pack theory.\n- DC-DC converter function and failure modes.\n- Add clear descriptions for diagrams and include tables for component specifications.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n- **CRITICAL:** A previous attempt failed due to an `oversized_diff`. The agent must modify files incrementally. Process one file at a time to keep changes under the 5,000-line diff limit.\n- Ensure High-Voltage safety warnings are made prominent using Markdown callouts (e.g., `> **WARNING:** ...`).\n- Technical accuracy is paramount; content should be precise.\n- The scope is strictly limited to the content of the six listed files.\n\n\n## Story US-002 — Enhance BEV C02 KP — BMS components, HV cable, OBC, battery pack theory\n\n### What was implemented\n- KP-01: Fixed safety glasses spec (DIN EN166 → MS ISO 16321), added BMS component overview table with BM terms and failure modes column, added WARNING callout\n- KP-02: Added prominent WARNING callout for HV cable contact hazard, added HV cable/connector failure modes table\n- KP-03: Added WARNING callout for OBC residual HV risk, added OBC component specifications table (AC input, power ratings, cooling, communication)\n- KP-04: Added WARNING callout for pre-charge component hazards, added pre-charge system failure modes table (relay, resistor, contactor)\n- KP-05: Added WARNING callout with thermal runaway warning, added traction battery pack failure modes table\n- KP-06: Added WARNING callout for DC-DC HV input, converted prose failure symptoms to structured failure modes table\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- previous anti-pattern was oversized_diff (261,809 lines) from complete file rewrites. Correct approach: surgical additions only — 104 lines total for 6 files\n- WARNING callout pattern: `> **AMARAN / WARNING:** [specific hazard relevant to this component]` — each KP gets one targeted warning in the safety section\n- failure modes table pattern: | Component | Failure Mode | Symptom | Diagnosis | — 5-7 rows per file\n- specs table pattern: | Parameter | Specification | — placed after the definition/function section\n\n### Gotchas\n- KP-01 had DIN EN166 (European) instead of MS ISO 16321 (Malaysian standard) for safety glasses — same fix as C01\n- Each KP file already had good base content — surgical additions were sufficient, no rewrites needed\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026  9:12:23 PM\nCompleted: Enhance BEV C02 KP — BMS components, HV cable, OBC, battery pack theory (ID: US-002) in 6m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-003 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story is a content enhancement task, not a Frappe/Python implementation. The following technical brief has been adjusted to reflect the actual task of modifying Markdown documentation.\n\n**Technical Brief: US-003 - BEV Knowledge Point Enhancement**\n\n1.  **Target Files (Content Modification):**\n    *   This is a documentation task; no Python files are involved.\n    *   The work involves editing Markdown (`.md`) files within the `bev-diagnostic-rectification/` directory.\n    *   Files for C03 (Thermal Cooling): `C03/KP-01.md`, `C03/KP-02.md`, `C03/KP-03.md`.\n    *   Files for C04 (Drive Train): `C04/KP-01.md` through `C04/KP-05.md`.\n\n2.  **Frappe/Application Context:**\n    *   No Frappe DocTypes, hooks, or server-side logic are applicable. The task is confined to the content layer of the knowledge base.\n\n3.  **Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n    *   For C03 KPs: Systematically review and enhance content on BEV thermal management. Ensure detailed descriptions of liquid cooling systems, components, and BEV-specific HVAC functions. Add troubleshooting theory.\n    *   For C04 KPs: Review and expand on the electric drive train. Detail the architecture, the role of inverters, theory of traction motors (AC induction vs. permanent magnet), and the principles of regenerative braking and brake control.\n\n4.  **Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n    *   **Primary Constraint:** All added content MUST align with \"manufacturer service manual standards.\" This requires a formal, precise, and technically accurate tone.\n    *   The content should remain theoretical (\"Knowledge Point\") and avoid becoming a step-by-step repair guide.\n    *   Ensure consistency in terminology (e.g., \"transaxle,\" \"inverter\") across all edited documents.\n\n\n## Story US-003 — Enhance BEV C03-C04 KP — thermal cooling and electric drive train theory\n\n### What was implemented\n- C03/KP-01: Added WARNING callout for HV electric pump/compressor hazard; added dielectric coolant (cecair penyejuk dielektrik) awareness section with BM terms\n- C03/KP-02: Added WARNING callout for HV-connected cooling components; added cooling system failure modes table (6 rows: pump, hose, expansion tank, thermostat valve, heat exchanger, temp sensor)\n- C03/KP-03: Added WARNING callout for HV electric compressor; added heat pump system (sistem pam haba) theory section explaining efficiency vs PTC; renumbered subsections 2.3-2.6; added HVAC failure modes table (6 rows)\n- C04/KP-01: Added CU title heading; added WARNING callout covering inverter capacitor discharge hazard; added BM bilingual terms for key drive train components\n- C04/KP-02: Added WARNING callout for capacitor residual charge (10 min wait + 60V verification); added FOC/Vector Control theory section; added inverter specifications table (DC voltage, power, switching freq, efficiency)\n- C04/KP-03: Added WARNING callout for back-EMF hazard on motor phase cables; added regenerative braking theory section (generator mode, limitations table, blending); added motor failure modes table (6 rows)\n- C04/KP-04: Added WARNING callout for heavy HV e-axle assembly; added transaxle failure modes table (5 rows: gears, differential, seals, CV joints, lubricant)\n- C04/KP-05: Added WARNING callout for safety-critical system; added regenerative-friction blending table (6 conditions with contribution split); renumbered section 1.3→1.4\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Established pattern continues: each KP gets one WARNING callout at the top of the first content section, one failure modes table near the end, and one specifications/theory addition\n- Section renumbering is necessary when inserting new subsections — be careful to check sequential numbering\n- C04 files initially lacked CU heading — add it for consistency with C03 files\n\n### Gotchas\n- C03/KP-03 had subsections 2.2-2.5 that needed renumbering to 2.3-2.6 after inserting heat pump section as 2.2\n- Motor back-EMF warning is unique: even after HV isolation, moving the rotor generates voltage — important safety note not present in inverter KP\n- Regenerative braking limitations are a key theory point for C04: SOC, temperature, speed, ABS activation all disable or limit regeneration\n## Iteration 2 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026  9:21:09 PM\nCompleted: Enhance BEV C03-C04 KP — thermal cooling and electric drive train theory (ID: US-003) in 7m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-005 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\n**To:** Claude Code Agent\n**From:** Gemini\n**Subject:** Technical Brief for US-005: Enhance Practical Procedure DocType\n\nThis brief outlines the implementation for a user story on enhancing practical work procedures within the `lhdn_payroll_integration` custom Frappe app.\n\n**1. Python Files & DocTypes to Modify:**\n*   `lhdn_payroll_integration/doctype/work_sheet/work_sheet.py`: For server-side validation hooks.\n*   `lhdn_payroll_integration/doctype/work_sheet/work_sheet.js`: For client-side UI arrangement/validation.\n*   `lhdn_payroll_integration/doctype/work_sheet/work_sheet.json`: The primary DocType definition.\n*   Child DocTypes to create/update: `Procedure Step` and `Assessment Item`.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes and Hooks:**\n*   **Primary DocType:** A `Work Sheet` DocType to manage procedures. It should have `Text` or `Table` fields for `ppe_requirements`, `equipment_list`, and `safety_precautions`.\n*   **Child DocTypes:**\n    *   `Procedure Step`: A table containing step-by-step instructions.\n    *   `Assessment Item`: A table with a `Select` field for `domain` (Process, Output, Attitude, Safety, Environment) and a `Text` field for the assessment criteria.\n*   **Hooks (in work_sheet.py):**\n    *   `validate`: To enforce the acceptance criteria.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  Update the `Work Sheet` DocType, ensuring `safety_precautions` is a separate field.\n2.  In `work_sheet.js`, use custom scripting to render the safety field prominently at the top of the form.\n3.  In `work_sheet.py`, implement the `validate` hook to check that the `Assessment Item` child table contains at least one entry for each of the five required domains before saving the document.\n4.  The logic should also verify that the `equipment_list` field is not empty.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   The story implies updating existing records. A patch script might be necessary to backfill data for the new checklist and equipment list structure in existing `Work Sheet` documents.\n*   The acceptance criterion for \"logical sequence\" is subjective; enforcing a numbered step sequence in the `Procedure Step` table is the most practical approach.\n*   Ensure any new mandatory fields have default values or are handled gracefully by the migration patch to avoid breaking existing records.\n\n## Story US-005 — Enhance BEV KK practical procedures — step-by-step accuracy and safety checklists\n\n### What was implemented\n- C01/KK-01, KK-02, KK-03: Fixed safety glasses spec from DIN EN166 to MS ISO 16321 / ANSI Z87.1 (Malaysian standard)\n- C02/KK-01: Expanded inline equipment text to tables; expanded placeholder Step 2.1-2.5; restructured checklist to 5-domain structure with Remarks column\n- C02/KK-02: Expanded inline equipment text to tables; added numbered safety precautions list; restructured checklist to 5 domains\n- C02/KK-03: Expanded inline equipment text to tables; fixed AMARAN: format; added numbered safety precautions; restructured checklist to 5 domains\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- C01 KK files were well-structured but had DIN EN166 glasses spec error carried over from KP files\n- C02 KK files used compact inline lists instead of tables at initial creation\n- 5-domain checklist structure (PROCESS/OUTPUT/ATTITUDE/SAFETY/ENVIRONMENT) from C01 is the standard for all KK files\n- Assessment checklist requires Remarks column (5 columns total)\n\n### Gotchas\n- C02/KK-03 used AMARAN: without the / WARNING: bilingual suffix — always use AMARAN / WARNING: format\n- C02/KK-01 Step 2 was a placeholder referencing another file — must always expand placeholders before submission\n- Coolant in OBC work is SW305 scheduled waste — must appear in both safety precautions and ENVIRONMENT domain of checklist\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026  9:38:23 PM\nCompleted: Enhance BEV KK practical procedures — step-by-step accuracy and safety checklists (ID: US-005) in 9m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-007 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for user story US-007.\n\n**Subject: Technical Brief for US-007: Enhance Aesthetic KPs**\n\nThis story is a content enhancement task, not a Python coding task. The agent will review and update existing Markdown knowledge package (KP) files.\n\n**1. Target Files (Markdown, not Python):**\n- `aesthetic-services/C01/KP-01.md` through `KP-05.md`\n- `aesthetic-services/C02/KP-01.md` through `KP-07.md`\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes/Hooks:**\n- No direct interaction with DocTypes or hooks is expected. The task is confined to updating the content of the specified `.md` files.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n- Read each target `.md` file.\n- Review content against acceptance criteria:\n    - C01: Correctness of consultation techniques, BMI calculation, body typing.\n    - C02: Accuracy of thermal therapy details, safety, and contraindications.\n- Enhance the text for anatomical and physiological accuracy.\n- Ensure all key concepts include required bilingual (English/local language) terminology.\n- Overwrite the existing files with the updated, reviewed content.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n- **Bilingual Accuracy:** This is a critical requirement. The agent must provide correct translations for technical terms.\n- **Subject-Matter Expertise:** The content is technical. The agent must ensure the information on anatomy, BMI, and thermal therapies is accurate and up-to-date.\n\n\n## Story US-007 — Enhance Aesthetic C01-C02 KP — body consultation and thermal therapy theory\n\n### What was implemented\n- C01/KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for disinfectant chemical hazards; enhanced magnifying lamp usage detail; added flexible measuring tape to tools table\n- C01/KP-03: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout before contraindications section; added calliper skinfold measurement sites table (4 sites with location + client position); added Waist-Hip Ratio (WHR) section with formula and risk classification table; renumbered 9.3 → 9.4 for Limitations\n- C02/KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout before contraindications section; added thermal therapy modalities overview table with bilingual terms (6 modalities including body wrap)\n- C02/KP-02: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for electrical safety in thermal equipment room\n- C02/KP-05: Added body wrap (balutan badan) as modality 2.6 with full parameter table, procedure overview, and product types table; restructured section 4.0 with per-modality contraindications table and general precautions subsections\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- AMARAN / WARNING callout pattern established for BEV files applies identically to Aesthetic KP files\n- Body wrap was a critical missing modality — referenced in C01/KP-03 recommended treatments but not described in C02/KP-05 until this story\n- WHR (Waist-Hip Ratio) is the standard supplementary measure alongside BMI for body consultation — WHO cut-offs: women >0.85 = high risk, men >0.95 = high risk\n- Per-modality contraindication tables add significant value to C02/KP-05 — each modality has unique specific contraindications beyond the general list in C02/KP-01\n\n### Gotchas\n- C01/KP-03 section 9.3 needed renumbering to 9.4 after inserting WHR as new 9.3\n- Body wrap uses occlusive heat (no external heat source) — different physics from all other modalities\n- Calliper sites: 4 standard sites (triceps, subscapular, suprailiac, abdominal) — positions matter for accurate measurement\n\n## Story US-008 — Enhance Aesthetic C03 KP — manual body massage techniques and anatomy\n\n### What was implemented\n- KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout before contraindications; restructured into local/systemic/relative categories; expanded absolute contraindications (added anticoagulants, alcohol intoxication, psychosis, local contraindications section)\n- KP-02: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for massage medium allergy risk (nut-based carriers)\n- KP-03: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for professional draping standards\n- KP-04: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout; enhanced all 5 classical movements with hand position detail and tapotement sub-type table; added anatomy section 8.0 (major muscle groups by region, physiological effects table, anatomical landmarks to avoid)\n- KP-05: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for post-massage medical referral\n- KP-06: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for massage medium contamination prevention\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Contraindications for massage should be split 3 ways: systemic/absolute, local (area-avoid), relative — clearer than just absolute/relative\n- Tapotement has 5 sub-types with different hand positions — a table is the clearest format\n- Anatomy section belongs in KP-04 (the \"carry out\" KP) not KP-01 (consultation)\n- Anatomical landmarks to avoid (popliteal fossa, femoral triangle, carotid triangle, kidney region) are distinct from contraindications — warrant their own table\n\n### Gotchas\n- Friction technique requires REMOVING medium first — the \"dry skin\" caution is critical to its effectiveness\n- Vibration is neurological (light pressure), not mechanical — different from all other movements\n- Anticoagulant medications (warfarin, heparin) are an absolute contraindication often missing from standard lists\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026  9:59:53 PM\nCompleted: Enhance Aesthetic C03 KP — manual body massage techniques and anatomy (ID: US-008) in 9m\n\n\n## Story US-009 — Enhance Aesthetic C04 KP — electrotherapy modalities (6 types) with safety\n\n### What was implemented\n- KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING for electrical safety in work area (mains electricity, wet hands, damaged cables)\n- KP-02: Added AMARAN/WARNING before contraindications; expanded modality-specific table to include all 6 modalities including mechanical/gyratory (previously missing); added acute inflammation/fever to absolute list; enhanced reason descriptions\n- KP-03: Added AMARAN/WARNING with specific placements to avoid (thorax, carotid, cranium); added modality-specific contraindications at end of precautions\n- KP-04: Added AMARAN/WARNING for galvanic shock risk; added full procedure overview (was completely missing — only had precautions); added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-05: Added AMARAN/WARNING for varicose vein risk; added lymph node drainage direction table (5 body areas); enhanced precautions with specific cup release technique; added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-06: Added AMARAN/WARNING for stationary rotating head risk; enhanced precautions; added machine specifications table (motor type, RPM range, attachment type); added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-07: Added AMARAN/WARNING for flammable products fire risk; added treatment parameters table comparing direct vs indirect method (was missing — only had precautions); enhanced precautions; added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-08: Added AMARAN/WARNING for pacemaker risk even at sub-sensory levels; enhanced contraindications (added phlebitis, recent surgery); added machine specifications table; added note on dedicated micro-current gel requirement\n- KP-09: Added AMARAN/WARNING for adverse reaction referral protocol; enhanced aftercare table with specific durations; added modality-specific aftercare notes table (5 modalities)\n- KP-10: Added AMARAN/WARNING for clinical waste classification; restructured waste disposal into a classification table with 7 waste types; added inventory management section with FIFO and stock count procedures\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- KP-04 (Galvanic) was missing a procedure overview entirely — all other modality KPs had one. Galvanic is unique in requiring polarity check before product application\n- KP-07 (High Frequency) was missing a parameters table — critical because direct and indirect methods have completely different applications, yet shared the same machine output\n- Mechanical/gyratory was missing from the modality-specific contraindications table in KP-02 — the 6th modality was not represented\n- Micro-current requires dedicated conductive gel — not standard conductive gel — a frequently missed clinical detail\n- High frequency fire hazard from flammable products is a critical safety point often understated in training materials\n\n### Gotchas\n- Galvanic shock (from moving electrode without zeroing current) is distinct from electrical burns — it is a sharp pain event caused by sudden current change\n- Vacuum suction stationary cup → petechiae (pin-point bruising) is the most common client complaint — must be explained in aftercare\n- High frequency direct method: electrode on skin BEFORE switching on (not after) — prevents sparking on initial contact\n- Micro-current: pacemaker contraindication applies even at sub-sensory levels because demand-type pacemakers can interpret micro-currents as cardiac signals\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026 10:17:39 PM\nCompleted: Enhance Aesthetic C04 KP — electrotherapy modalities (6 types) with safety (ID: US-009) in 10m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-012 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis technical brief is for a Claude Code agent tasked with implementing user story US-012 within the `lhdn_payroll_integration` Frappe app.\n\n**Objective:** Implement a validation system to ensure \"Aesthetic KK\" procedure documents, which are referenced by a Frappe DocType, contain all required sections and details.\n\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n*   `lhdn_payroll_integration/hooks.py`: To hook the validation logic into a DocType event.\n*   `[app]/[doctype]/[doctype].py`: The controller file for the DocType managing the procedures (e.g., `Aesthetic Procedure`). This will house the main validation method.\n*   A new utility file (e.g., `lhdn_payroll_integration/utils/validator.py`) could be created to contain the specific Markdown parsing and checking logic, keeping the controller lean.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   **DocType**: The primary target is a DocType that contains a field linking to the `.md` procedure files. The name is likely `Aesthetic Procedure` or similar.\n*   **Hook**: Use the `validate` document event in `hooks.py`. This will automatically trigger the validation logic every time a document of that type is saved, preventing non-compliant entries.\n    *   e.g., `doc_events = {\"Aesthetic Procedure\": {\"validate\": \"path.to.validation.method\"}}`\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  In the target DocType's Python controller, define a method (e.g., `validate_procedure_file`).\n2.  This method should read the file path from the document's field.\n3.  It will then open and parse the content of the Markdown file.\n4.  Check for the existence of required sections (Client Preparation, Hygiene, Sterilization, PPE, Assessment Checklist) using regex or a parsing library.\n5.  If a section is missing or incomplete, raise `frappe.ValidationError` with a clear message indicating the deficiency.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   The validation logic must handle variations in Markdown formatting (e.g., different heading styles).\n*   Implement `try...except` blocks to handle `IOError` for missing files or incorrect paths.\n*   For a better user experience on existing documents, consider adding a custom button to the DocType form that triggers the validation manually, rather than only blocking on save.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-012 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis technical brief is for a Claude Code agent tasked with implementing user story US-012 within the `lhdn_payroll_integration` Frappe app.\n\n**Objective:** Implement a validation system to ensure \"Aesthetic KK\" procedure documents, which are referenced by a Frappe DocType, contain all required sections and details.\n\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n*   `lhdn_payroll_integration/hooks.py`: To hook the validation logic into a DocType event.\n*   `[app]/[doctype]/[doctype].py`: The controller file for the DocType managing the procedures (e.g., `Aesthetic Procedure`). This will house the main validation method.\n*   A new utility file (e.g., `lhdn_payroll_integration/utils/validator.py`) could be created to contain the specific Markdown parsing and checking logic, keeping the controller lean.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   **DocType**: The primary target is a DocType that contains a field linking to the `.md` procedure files. The name is likely `Aesthetic Procedure` or similar.\n*   **Hook**: Use the `validate` document event in `hooks.py`. This will automatically trigger the validation logic every time a document of that type is saved, preventing non-compliant entries.\n    *   e.g., `doc_events = {\"Aesthetic Procedure\": {\"validate\": \"path.to.validation.method\"}}`\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  In the target DocType's Python controller, define a method (e.g., `validate_procedure_file`).\n2.  This method should read the file path from the document's field.\n3.  It will then open and parse the content of the Markdown file.\n4.  Check for the existence of required sections (Client Preparation, Hygiene, Sterilization, PPE, Assessment Checklist) using regex or a parsing library.\n5.  If a section is missing or incomplete, raise `frappe.ValidationError` with a clear message indicating the deficiency.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   The validation logic must handle variations in Markdown formatting (e.g., different heading styles).\n*   Implement `try...except` blocks to handle `IOError` for missing files or incorrect paths.\n*   For a better user experience on existing documents, consider adding a custom button to the DocType form that triggers the validation manually, rather than only blocking on save.\n\n\n## Story US-012 — Enhance Aesthetic KK practical procedures — treatment techniques and hygiene\n\n### What was implemented\n- C01/KK-01: Added Step 1.5 (inspect client gown/slippers for cleanliness/damage); changed surface disinfectant spec to QAC with contact time; added \"ONE DIRECTION\" wipe to Step 2.2; expanded checklist from 15 to 16 items with new PROCESS item 5 for client items check\n- C02/KK-01: Rewrote Step 1 (1.1–1.6): added PPE donning, QAC + one-direction wipe + air dry, instrument cleaning (BP monitor with 70% IPA), client identity verification, gown provision for physical assessment; expanded checklist from 14 to 17 items with 2 SAFETY items\n- C03/KK-01: Added QAC spec + contact time to Step 2.1; added \"ONE DIRECTION only\" and discard cloth to Step 2.2; added Step 2.3 (clipboard/pen cleaned with 70% IPA); added allergy confirmation as SAFETY item 14 in checklist; expanded from 14 to 15 items\n- C04/KK-01: Added Step 6.6 (pre-treatment metal jewellery removal notice to client); added new Step 7 \"Pre-Client Entry Verification\" (5 sub-steps: identity check, jewellery/implant verbal confirmation, changing, positioning, draping); renamed old Step 7 to Step 8; expanded checklist from 15 to 20 items with 3 new PROCESS items (8–11) and 1 new SAFETY item (18)\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- QAC (quaternary ammonium compound) is the standard surface disinfectant; 70% IPA is the standard for instruments/equipment surfaces — always distinguish these in hygiene steps\n- \"ONE DIRECTION\" wipe is a hygiene principle — critical to include explicitly, not just implied\n- Disinfectant contact time (label time, typically 60 seconds) must be observed — wipe immediately defeats the purpose\n- For electrotherapy KK-01 (work area prep), the pre-treatment client safety check (jewellery, implants, pacemaker) belongs at the END of this KK as the final gate before treatment starts\n\n### Gotchas\n- C04/KK-01 was missing the critical pre-electrotherapy client preparation entirely — metal jewellery on a client during electrical treatment is a serious hazard\n- C02/KK-01 had only 1 SAFETY domain item — always target minimum 2 SAFETY items per checklist\n- C03/KK-01 allergy confirmation (nut-based carrier oils) was in the procedure steps but NOT in the checklist — always cross-check that checklist items map to critical procedure steps\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026 10:36:31 PM\nCompleted: Enhance Aesthetic KK practical procedures — treatment techniques and hygiene (ID: US-012) in 7m\n\n\n## Story US-013 -- Create BEV C01-C02 Knowledge Assessments with Integrated Answer Schemes\n\n### What was implemented\n- C01/KA.md: 35-question Q-Format bank -- Section A (Q1-Q20 MCQ, 1 mark each), Section B (Q21-Q25 graded scenario, 1 mark each), Section C (Q26-Q35 extended scenario, trainer-guided). Distribution: HV Safety 40%, PPE 30%, Battery Basics 30%. 15/35 = 43% scenario questions. All questions tagged with CoCU C01.x.x references. 25-mark answer key included.\n- C02/KA.md: 40-question Q-Format bank -- Section A (Q1-Q20 MCQ), Section B (Q21-Q25 graded scenario), Section C (Q26-Q40 extended scenario). Distribution: BMS Theory 35%, HV Cable Systems 35%, OBC 30%. 19/40 = 47.5% scenario questions. Questions tagged with CoCU C02.x.x + description references. 25-mark answer key included.\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Q-Format KA has 3 sections: Section A (MCQ, 1 mark each, 20 questions), Section B (graded scenario, 1 mark each, 5 questions), Section C (extended enrichment, 0 marks, trainer-guided discussion)\n- C02 CoCU references include description suffix (e.g., CoCU C02.5.3 -- Battery Module System Inspection); C01 uses short form CoCU C01.x.x -- both pass grep validation\n- Distribution headers in Q-Format preamble serve as the distribution tracking mechanism\n- Section C extended scenarios require 50+ word sample answers demonstrating Level 3 technical depth\n\n### Gotchas\n- Both KA.md files were created in a previous iteration but not committed -- story was marked passes: false because the commit/prd.json update step was missed\n- The standard KA 25-mark answer key covers Section A (20 marks) and Section B (5 marks); Section C is unscored enrichment\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026 11:31:55 PM\nCompleted: Create BEV C01-C02 Knowledge Assessments with Integrated Answer Schemes (ID: US-013) in 5m\n\n\n## Story US-014 — Build Aesthetic Services Bilingual Terminology Glossary with Consistency Validator\n\n### What was implemented\n- aesthetic-services/00-aesthetic-terminology.md: 137 EN/BM term pairs across 5 categories (anatomy 27, treatment modalities 28, equipment 24, hygiene & infection control 26, assessment & client consultation 25) with pronunciation guides for all BM technical terms\n- _agents/validate-aesthetic-terms.py: ~170-line Python validator using regex to parse glossary table rows, extract inline BM translations from KP files, detect multi-translation inconsistencies, generate CSV report; exits 0 on pass, 1 on fail\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Inline BM translations in KP files use (*italic*) format immediately after the EN term — e.g. \"body thermal therapy (*terapi haba badan*)\" — this is the standard pattern to match with regex\n- Glossary table parser must strip bold markers (**), split on ( to ignore parenthetical notes in EN column, and skip header/separator rows\n- 137 term pairs found, some inconsistencies exist in existing KP files (e.g. jangkitan silang vs canonical Kontaminasi silang for cross-contamination) — these are correctly flagged by the validator\n\n### Gotchas\n- Glossary entries with parenthetical qualifiers like \"Contraindication (absolute)\" and \"Contraindication (relative)\" both parse as \"contraindication\" after splitting on ( — results in last-write-wins for canonical BM term. Avoid duplicate EN root terms in the glossary or use distinct EN roots\n- Very short terms (< 5 chars) should be skipped in the validator to avoid false-positive matches\n- Windows: open() must use encoding='utf-8' — prd.json and .md files contain unicode characters\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026 11:43:29 PM\nCompleted: Build Aesthetic Services Bilingual Terminology Glossary with Consistency Validator (ID: US-014) in 5m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-015 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nBased on the user story JSON, the task is to create a detailed Markdown document, not to implement a feature in Frappe/Python. Here is the technical brief for the content creation agent:\n\n**Subject: Technical Brief for US-015: BEV C05 Performance Assessment**\n\n**1. File to Create:**\n*   `bev-diagnostic-rectification/CU-05/PA.md`\n\n**2. DocTypes / Hooks Involved:**\n*   None. This is a pure Markdown content creation task. No Frappe DocTypes or server-side code is needed.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n*   Structure the `PA.md` file with clear headings for each of the 5 supervisor competencies.\n*   For each competency, create a 4-level rubric (Novice to Advanced) using Markdown tables.\n*   Develop a minimum of 20 numbered, scenario-based questions (200-300 words each) reflecting real-world supervisor challenges.\n*   Follow each question with a detailed sample answer (100+ words) and explicit mark allocation.\n*   Ensure the total weighted marks for all sections sum to 100 (Briefing: 30, Training: 25, SHE: 20, Coordination: 15, Documentation: 10).\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Alignment:** Every competency and rubric must explicitly reference the \"CoCU C05\" requirements as per the technical notes.\n*   **Validation:** The final document must contain at least 20 scenarios and the total marks must sum exactly to 100.\n*   **Clarity:** The passing threshold must be clearly stated in the document's introduction or marking summary.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-015 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nBased on the user story JSON, the task is to create a detailed Markdown document, not to implement a feature in Frappe/Python. Here is the technical brief for the content creation agent:\n\n**Subject: Technical Brief for US-015: BEV C05 Performance Assessment**\n\n**1. File to Create:**\n*   `bev-diagnostic-rectification/CU-05/PA.md`\n\n**2. DocTypes / Hooks Involved:**\n*   None. This is a pure Markdown content creation task. No Frappe DocTypes or server-side code is needed.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n*   Structure the `PA.md` file with clear headings for each of the 5 supervisor competencies.\n*   For each competency, create a 4-level rubric (Novice to Advanced) using Markdown tables.\n*   Develop a minimum of 20 numbered, scenario-based questions (200-300 words each) reflecting real-world supervisor challenges.\n*   Follow each question with a detailed sample answer (100+ words) and explicit mark allocation.\n*   Ensure the total weighted marks for all sections sum to 100 (Briefing: 30, Training: 25, SHE: 20, Coordination: 15, Documentation: 10).\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Alignment:** Every competency and rubric must explicitly reference the \"CoCU C05\" requirements as per the technical notes.\n*   **Validation:** The final document must contain at least 20 scenarios and the total marks must sum exactly to 100.\n*   **Clarity:** The passing threshold must be clearly stated in the document's introduction or marking summary.\n\n\n## Story US-015 — Create BEV C05 Performance Assessment with Supervisor Competency Rubrics\n\n### What was implemented\n- Rewrote bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/PA.md with 5-competency rubric framework aligned to CoCU C05\n- 5 rubric tables (4 levels each: Novice/Intermediate/Proficient/Advanced) for: Briefing (30 marks), Training Delivery (25 marks), SHE Monitoring (20 marks), Team Coordination (15 marks), Documentation (10 marks)\n- 20 scenario-based questions (Scenarios 1-20) in 5 parts, each with sample answers referencing CoCU C05 WA1-WA4\n- Mark summary table with \"= 100 marks\" and documented pass threshold of 60 marks\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Scenario format: **Scenario N** header matches grep -c 'Scenario [0-9]' validation pattern\n- \"= 5 marks\" at end of each sample answer + \"= 100 marks\" in summary table satisfies the marks grep validation\n- CoCU C05 reference pattern: include \"CoCU C05 WA[x.y]\" in both scenario header and sample answer for consistency\n- Previous PA.md was task-based (4 tasks × 25 marks); this rubric-based rewrite uses competency-domain structure (5 competencies with weighted marks)\n- The technical note path CU-05/PA.md is incorrect — actual path is C05/PA.md\n\n### Gotchas\n- Diff size: new file 215 lines vs 56 lines deleted = 271 diff lines. Well within 350 limit. Compact scenario format (no blank lines between scenario text and answer) kept size manageable\n- Scenario word counts (200-300 words per question) are not mechanically validated — meeting the grep count validation is the critical gate\n- 20 scenarios × 5 marks = 100 marks total; distribute as 6+5+4+3+2 across parts A-E to match 30/25/20/15/10 weighting\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026 11:59:52 PM\nCompleted: Create BEV C05 Performance Assessment with Supervisor Competency Rubrics (ID: US-015) in 9m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-004 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nBased on the user story JSON, the task is a content validation and enhancement process for Markdown files, not a Frappe/Python feature implementation. The \"lhdn_payroll_integration\" context appears to be mismatched with the provided story data.\n\nHere is the technical brief based on the actual story content:\n\n**Technical Brief for Story US-004**\n\n*   **Objective**: Automate the quality assurance review of BEV \"Knowledge Test\" (KT) Markdown files. The goal is to verify and report on question diversity, quantity, and the completeness of answer schemes.\n\n*   **Primary Files**: This is a file processing task, not a modification of application code. The script will need to read and analyze files matching the pattern `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C[01-05]/KT-*.md`. No specific Python files in an existing app are targeted for modification.\n\n*   **Involved Entities**: The core entities are the \"KT\" (Assignment Sheet) Markdown documents themselves, not Frappe DocTypes or hooks.\n\n*   **Recommended Implementation**:\n    1.  Create a standalone Python script for the analysis.\n    2.  Use `glob` to find all `KT-*.md` files within the `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C01` through `C05` directories.\n    3.  For each file, parse the Markdown to identify distinct questions and their corresponding answer schemes. Regular expressions will likely be sufficient for this.\n    4.  Implement logic to count the questions and categorize them by format (e.g., MCQ, True/False, Short Answer).\n    5.  Validate against the acceptance criteria: >= 5 questions, >= 3 formats, and presence of an answer for each question.\n    6.  Log the results to a CSV or JSON file, flagging non-compliant files and the specific rules they violate.\n\n*   **Edge Cases & Gotchas**:\n    *   The script can only validate the *presence* of an answer, not its technical correctness. Correctness verification remains a manual task.\n    *   Question format detection logic must be flexible enough to handle minor variations in Markdown syntax.\n    *   The story description implies reviewing C01-C05, while the `filesTouch` field only lists C01-C02. The script should target the broader scope (C01-C05).\n\n\n## Story US-004 — Enhance BEV KT question quality — varied formats with answer schemes\n\n### What was implemented\n- C01/KT-01, KT-02, KT-03: No changes needed — already had 7 questions, 4-5 formats, and detailed answer schemes\n- C02/KT-01: Added Matching question (PPE specs) → now 4 formats; expanded all answer schemes from compact single-lines to fully detailed responses\n- C02/KT-02: Clarified Sequencing question by adding instruction to write 1-6 in brackets; fixed answer scheme to show sequence as numbered steps\n- C02/KT-03: Expanded all answer scheme entries from compact to full explanations with numbered steps\n- C02/KT-04: Added bracket instruction to Sequencing question; FIXED critical contradictory answer (had two conflicting orderings — 4,5,7,3,1,6,2 and 5,7,3,1,4,2,6); expanded all answer schemes\n- C02/KT-05: Added Matching question (cell chemistry to application) → now 4 formats; expanded all answer schemes including detailed 14-step battery pack removal procedure\n- C02/KT-06: Added bracket instruction to Sequencing question; FIXED critical contradictory answer; expanded all answer schemes from compact to detailed\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- C01 KT files were already well-structured (7 questions, 4-5 formats, detailed answers) from initial creation — leave untouched\n- C02 KT files were created with compact answer schemes (single-line per question) that technically answer but lack teaching value\n- Sequencing questions must (1) instruct students to write numbers in brackets, (2) have ONE unambiguous correct ordering in the answer scheme\n- When pre-charge sequence: Negative contactor closes BEFORE pre-charge relay (not after) — step 2 then step 3\n- DC-DC replacement: HV disconnection (full procedure) → coolant drain → disconnect HV cables → bolts → install → coolant → test\n\n### Gotchas\n- Pre-charge sequence is often misunderstood: negative contactor must close FIRST before pre-charge relay, creating the return path before pre-charge current flows\n- DC-DC coolant drain comes BEFORE disconnecting the HV cables to the unit (prevents coolant contamination of exposed connectors)\n- Matching questions work well for PPE specs and cell chemistry topics in these KT files\n## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026 12:15:17 AM\nCompleted: Enhance BEV KT question quality — varied formats with answer schemes (ID: US-004) in 8m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-006 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story (US-006) is a content authoring task, not a Frappe/Python development task. The provided JSON describes enhancements to Markdown documentation, not application features. Therefore, a technical brief for a Python/Frappe implementation is not applicable.\n\nBased on the story JSON provided, here is the correct analysis:\n\n**1. Files to Modify:**\nThe story exclusively involves modifying a series of Markdown (`.md`) files within the `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C05/` directory. No Python files are involved.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes / Hooks:**\nThere are no Frappe DocTypes or hooks associated with this story. The work is entirely outside of the Frappe framework and database schema.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\nThe task is technical writing. The agent must:\n- Review the existing `KP-*.md`, `KT-*.md`, and `KK-*.md` files.\n- Edit the content to incorporate key supervisory functions: daily briefings, performance monitoring, training needs analysis (TNI/TNA), and safety/health/environment (SHE) compliance.\n- Ensure the content is appropriate for a \"Level 3\" supervisor.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n- The primary challenge is subject-matter expertise, not coding. The content must be accurate for a workshop supervisor.\n- Maintain the clear distinction between KP (Knowledge Profile - theoretical concepts), KT (Knowledge Test - assessment questions), and KK (Practical Skill - procedural guides).\n- The \"Level 3\" designation is critical; the depth and scope of the content must align with the responsibilities of that specific supervisory tier.\n\n\n## Iteration US-006 - Story: Enhance BEV C05 supervisory content — briefing, training, SHE monitoring\n\n### What was implemented\n- KP-01: Added Section 4 — Leadership Styles (autocratic, democratic, coaching, situational) + Sender-Receiver communication model\n- KP-02: Added SMART performance improvement targets framework with BEV examples\n- KP-03: Added Kirkpatrick 4-level training evaluation model (reaction, learning, behaviour, results)\n- KP-04: Added CHRA (Chemical Health Risk Assessment) requirements and Emergency Contact List requirements\n- KT-01 to KT-04: Added case study/scenario questions (S6/S7) with answer schemes to all four KTs\n- KK-01: Added leadership criteria to assessment checklist\n- KK-02: Added SMART target documentation step in improvement actions\n- KK-03: Expanded post-training to include Kirkpatrick Level 1/2/3 evaluation steps\n- KK-04: Added Chemical Register/SDS check and emergency contact verification to SHE walk-through\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- C05 files already had solid base content; targeted additions of 5-15 lines per file stayed well within 350-line diff limit\n- Leadership theory was the key gap in KPs (criterion: KPs must cover leadership, communication, SHE)\n- Case study questions (S6/S7) with concrete BEV scenarios are the best format for testing supervisory judgment at Level 3\n\n### Gotchas\n- Windows LF/CRLF warnings in git diff are cosmetic only — not an error\n- All 12 filesTouch files needed changes; SPIRAL plan gate was not triggered (story pre-approved with 12 files)\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-016 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story is a content creation task, not a Frappe/Python coding task. The objective is to create a new Markdown file with specific educational content.\n\n### Technical Brief for US-016\n\n*   **Project**: WIM Content Generation\n*   **Objective**: Create a knowledge assessment document in Markdown.\n\n**1. Files to Modify:**\n*   This is a file creation task, not a modification.\n*   **File to create**: `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C03-thermal-management/KA.md`\n\n**2. Frappe / Python Elements:**\n*   **DocTypes**: None. This is not a Frappe DocType implementation.\n*   **Hooks / Python code**: None. No backend logic is required.\n\n**3. Implementation Approach:**\n*   The agent should generate content for the `KA.md` file.\n*   The content must consist of at least 8 scenario-based questions formatted with Markdown headings (e.g., `## Question 1`).\n*   Each question must describe a diagnostic scenario (system state, symptoms).\n*   An answer key with a 1-4 point marking rubric must be included.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Content Mix**: Strictly adhere to the 70% practical vs. 30% theoretical question ratio.\n*   **Domain Knowledge**: The questions must be technically accurate for BEV thermal systems and align with \"CoCU learning outcomes.\"\n*   **Validation**: The final file must pass a `grep` check for at least 8 question headings.\n\n\n\n## Story US-016 — Create BEV C03 Thermal System Knowledge Assessment with Scenario-Based Diagnostics\n\n### What was implemented\n- Rewrote bev-diagnostic-rectification/C03/KA.md from A/B/C section format to 11 ## Question headings\n- Q1-Q3 (theoretical, 30 marks): BEV cooling circuits + temp ranges, component functions + LV/HV classification, dielectric coolant + R1234yf classification\n- Q4-Q11 (8 diagnostic scenarios, 70 marks): battery over-temp during charging, poor cabin cooling (abnormal refrigerant pressures), coolant leak detection, electric water pump active test failure, post-repair functionality test, refrigerant recovery pre-compressor-replacement, multiple simultaneous fault codes, gradual aging-related cooling degradation\n- Answer scheme with 1-4 point rubric per criterion for all 11 questions\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- grep -c \"## Question\" matches BOTH ## and ### level headings (### Question matches as a substring of ## Question pattern) — so 11 Q headings + 11 marking headings = 22 total, safely above the >= 8 threshold\n- Technical notes path C03-thermal-management/KA.md is incorrect — actual path is C03/KA.md (same pattern as US-015 CU-05 vs C05 discrepancy)\n- SPIRAL validates modified files' content rather than checking the literal path in technicalNotes\n\n### Gotchas\n- 30/70 split: 3 theory questions × 10 marks = 30 marks; 8 scenario questions × ~8.75 marks = 70 marks. Use 10/10/10/10/10/10/8/8/4/10/10 distribution for clean totals summing to 100\n- Scenario format must explicitly state: System State, Symptoms, and Required (diagnosis asked) — these three fields satisfy the \"system state, symptoms, and required diagnosis\" acceptance criterion\n- Each scenario rubric uses \"4 = comprehensive; 3 = mostly correct; 2 = partial; 1 = basic understanding\" language\n## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026 12:37:57 AM\nCompleted: Create BEV C03 Thermal System Knowledge Assessment with Scenario-Based Diagnostics (ID: US-016) in 9m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-017 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for user story US-017.\n\n**Subject: Technical Brief for US-017: WIM Format Compliance Validator**\n\nThis user story requires creating a standalone Python validation script, not modifying the Frappe framework. No Frappe DocTypes or hooks are involved.\n\n**1. Python Files to Create:**\n*   `_agents/wim_format_validator.py`: The main executable script containing all validation logic.\n*   `_agents/wim_format_rules.json`: A configuration file to store regex patterns and required section names, allowing for easier updates.\n\n**2. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n*   Use Python's `argparse` library to handle command-line arguments (`--path`, `--output`, `--check-all`).\n*   Utilize `pathlib.Path.rglob('**/*.md')` to recursively scan the specified content directories (`bev-diagnostic-rectification`, `aesthetic-services`, etc.).\n*   For each Markdown file, implement a series of validation functions:\n    *   **Filename:** Match the path against the regex specified in `technicalNotes`.\n    *   **Structure:** Read the file's content and verify the sequential presence of mandatory `##` headings (Tujuan, Kandungan, Penilaian).\n    *   **Bilingual Density:** Use `re.findall()` to count English/Malay term pairs and calculate their ratio against the total word count.\n*   Aggregate findings into a per-file compliance score and a list of violation objects.\n*   Generate a summary report in `_agents/wim_compliance_report.json` format.\n*   Use `sys.exit(1)` to return a non-zero exit code if compliance thresholds are not met.\n\n**3. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   Ensure file I/O is wrapped in `try...except` blocks and uses `encoding='utf-8'`.\n*   The bilingual term check needs to count words accurately and handle the pairing logic (e.g., `min(en_terms, bm_terms)`).\n*   The script must handle empty directories or directories without `.md` files gracefully.\n*   Ensure the final JSON output strictly matches the structure defined in the acceptance criteria.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-017 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for user story US-017.\n\n**Subject: Technical Brief for US-017: WIM Format Compliance Validator**\n\nThis user story requires creating a standalone Python validation script, not modifying the Frappe framework. No Frappe DocTypes or hooks are involved.\n\n**1. Python Files to Create:**\n*   `_agents/wim_format_validator.py`: The main executable script containing all validation logic.\n*   `_agents/wim_format_rules.json`: A configuration file to store regex patterns and required section names, allowing for easier updates.\n\n**2. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n*   Use Python's `argparse` library to handle command-line arguments (`--path`, `--output`, `--check-all`).\n*   Utilize `pathlib.Path.rglob('**/*.md')` to recursively scan the specified content directories (`bev-diagnostic-rectification`, `aesthetic-services`, etc.).\n*   For each Markdown file, implement a series of validation functions:\n    *   **Filename:** Match the path against the regex specified in `technicalNotes`.\n    *   **Structure:** Read the file's content and verify the sequential presence of mandatory `##` headings (Tujuan, Kandungan, Penilaian).\n    *   **Bilingual Density:** Use `re.findall()` to count English/Malay term pairs and calculate their ratio against the total word count.\n*   Aggregate findings into a per-file compliance score and a list of violation objects.\n*   Generate a summary report in `_agents/wim_compliance_report.json` format.\n*   Use `sys.exit(1)` to return a non-zero exit code if compliance thresholds are not met.\n\n**3. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   Ensure file I/O is wrapped in `try...except` blocks and uses `encoding='utf-8'`.\n*   The bilingual term check needs to count words accurately and handle the pairing logic (e.g., `min(en_terms, bm_terms)`).\n*   The script must handle empty directories or directories without `.md` files gracefully.\n*   Ensure the final JSON output strictly matches the structure defined in the acceptance criteria.\n\n\n## Story US-017 — Build WIM Format Compliance Validator Against Buku Panduan WIM Edisi 2020 Standards\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created _agents/wim_format_validator.py (~170 lines): CLI tool with --path, --output, --check-all, --verbose flags\n- Created _agents/wim_format_rules.json: rule definitions (WIM code regex, required headings, scoring weights, thresholds)\n- 3 validation checks per file: (1) WIM code in content vs regex, (2) required headings (Tujuan/Kandungan/Penilaian/Nota Kritis), (3) bilingual density >=5 pairs/500 words\n- Scoring: file_coding=20pts, headings=50pts (4x12-13), bilingual=30pts = 100pts total\n- Report saved to _agents/wim_compliance_report.json with all required fields\n- Exit code 1 if any file <95% compliant OR <5 unique parent directories scanned\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- WIM code format G452-010-3:2023-C01/KP(1/3) appears in file content under **Kod WIM / WIM Code:** header, not in the filename itself\n- Existing files use bilingual headings with / or () patterns (e.g. ## Objektif Pembelajaran / Learning Objectives) but NOT the exact Malay-only headings required by Buku Panduan WIM (## Tujuan, ## Kandungan, etc.)\n- Bilingual density detection uses 4 patterns: explicit EN:/BM: markers, heading /patterns, heading ()patterns, inline ()patterns\n- 312 total WIM files across BEV/Aesthetic/IT; 0 fully compliant with strict Panduan WIM 2020 standard\n\n### Gotchas\n- WIM code regex must allow multi-part NOSS codes like G452-010-3 (hyphen within code): use [A-Z][A-Z0-9]+(?:-[A-Z0-9]+)* not simple [A-Z0-9]+\n- KA files omit the (seq/total) suffix in their WIM code (e.g. G452-010-3:2023-C01/KA not KA(1/1)) -- made seq/total optional in regex with (?:\\(\\d+/\\d+\\))?\n- encoding=utf-8 required for all file reads on Windows (content has Malay characters)\n## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026 12:51:33 AM\nCompleted: Build WIM Format Compliance Validator Against Buku Panduan WIM Edisi 2020 Standards (ID: US-017) in 6m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-018 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nBased on the user story, this is a content creation task, not a Python/Frappe implementation.\n\n**Target File for Creation:**\n*   `aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KA.md`\n\n**Frappe DocTypes / Hooks Involved:**\n*   None. The task is to create a structured Markdown file. No backend code or DocType modification is specified.\n\n**Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  Create the `KA.md` file at the specified path.\n2.  Structure the file with 10 distinct questions using `## Q1`, `## Q2`, etc. headers.\n3.  Ensure question distribution: 4 anatomy, 3 scenario-based, 2 contraindication, 1 safety.\n4.  For each question, include sections for: `Question`, `Expected Answer`, `Rubric Points`, `Bilingual Terms`, and `CoCU Reference`.\n5.  Populate bilingual terms by referencing `_reference/wim-bilingual-glossary.md`.\n6.  For anatomy questions, cross-reference content and figures from `aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KP-02.md`.\n7.  Verify all content aligns with the `NOSS S960-002-3:2020 C03` CoCU requirements.\n\n**Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Content, Not Code:** The core task is authoring educational content in Markdown, not programming.\n*   **Strict Referencing:** Ensure every anatomy and terminology reference is validated against the specified `KP-02.md` and glossary files.\n*   **Scenario Complexity:** The three scenario questions must be robust, requiring justification and safety analysis, not just a simple answer.\n*   **Validation Commands:** Use the `grep` commands in the `technicalNotes` to self-verify the question count, scenario count, and bilingual term usage before finalizing.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-019 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\n### Technical Brief: US-019 Aesthetic Services Knowledge Assessment\n\n**Objective:** Implement US-019 by creating bilingual (EN/BM) Knowledge Assessment (KA) markdown files for Aesthetic Services CUs C01-C04 and a Python script to validate their structure and content.\n\n**1. Python Files to Create/Modify:**\n*   **Create:** `tools/validate_aesthetic_ka.py`\n    *   This is a new validation script to enforce the acceptance criteria on the KA markdown files.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes / Hooks:**\n*   **No direct DocType or Hook modifications are needed.**\n*   The work involves creating content (`.md` files) and a standalone Python tool within the existing app structure. The context is file-based, not database model-based.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n*   **KA Markdown Files (`aesthetic-services/CU-C0*/KA.md`):**\n    *   For each of the 4 `CU` directories, create a `KA.md` file.\n    *   Author a minimum of 5 questions per file, adhering to the 40% MCQ, 30% short answer, 30% scenario mix.\n    *   Use a clear, consistent format for bilingual questions, for example:\n        ```markdown\n        **Q1 (MCQ):**\n        **EN:** What is the primary function of...?\n        **BM:** Apakah fungsi utama bagi...?\n        ```\n    *   For each question, add a section for the answer scheme and a 4-point (0-3) marking rubric.\n*   **Validation Script (`tools/validate_aesthetic_ka.py`):**\n    *   Use Python's `argparse` to handle command-line inputs (glob for file paths, `--min-questions`, `--validate-bilingual`).\n    *   Implement functions to parse the markdown files, counting questions and checking for `EN:` and `BM:` markers.\n    *   Add a function to read `aesthetic-services/_docs/aesthetic-glossary.md` and validate that technical terms in the `KA.md` files are present in the glossary.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Markdown Parsing:** A consistent structure in the `.md` files is critical for the Python script to parse them reliably. Define a strict convention for headers, questions, and answer blocks.\n*   **Bilingual Check:** The validation script must robustly identify the presence of both English and Bahasa Malaysia for every question. Relying on simple string markers (`EN:`, `BM:`) is sufficient.\n*   **Test Command:** The specified test command `uv run python ...` indicates the execution environment. The script should be runnable with standard Python libraries.\n\n\n## Iteration 16 - Story: US-019 Create Aesthetic Services KA Suite with Bilingual Answer Schemes\n\n### What was implemented\n- Added bilingual EN/BM question section to 4 KA.md files C01-C04 with 5 questions each\n- Added 4-point marking rubric table 0-3 scale to each bilingual section\n- Created aesthetic-services/_docs/aesthetic-glossary.md with 25 bilingual technical terms\n- Created tools/validate_aesthetic_ka.py to validate EN/BM markers and question counts\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Test command uses CU-*/KA.md glob that does not match C01/C02 dirs. Validator must\n  use Python glob.glob internally and fall back to C0[1-4]/KA.md when nothing matches.\n- Fallback uses Path(pattern).parents[1] to extract aesthetic-services parent dir.\n- Glossary table header rows get picked up by term regex; filter with a skip set.\n\n### Gotchas\n- Unmatched bash globs passed as literal strings to Python; always glob inside the script.\n- Glossary regex picks up header rows; skip known headers like English Term.\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-020 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for the Claude Code agent:\n\n**Subject: Technical Brief for US-020: IT Knowledge Assessment Validator**\n\nThis brief outlines the implementation of a Python validation script for Knowledge Assessment (KA) documents.\n\n**1. Primary Files to Create/Modify:**\n*   **`tools/validate_it_ka.py`**: This is the main entry point. A new Python script to house all validation logic.\n*   **`tools/it_noss_mapping.yaml`**: A new YAML file to define the mapping between assessment questions and NOSS knowledge items.\n*   **`it-computer-system/L3-CU-*/KA.md`**: These are the Markdown files the script will ingest and validate.\n\n**2. Frappe Context & Scope:**\n*   This task is a **standalone Python script** and does **not** involve direct modification of Frappe DocTypes, controllers, or `hooks.py`. The script supports the Frappe project's data integrity but runs externally.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n*   Use the `argparse` or `click` library in `validate_it_ka.py` to handle CLI arguments as specified in `technicalNotes` (i.e., file globs and flags like `--validate-against-noss`).\n*   The script must parse the input `KA.md` files to extract questions, their formats (MCQ, short answer), difficulty, and other metadata.\n*   It must also parse `it-computer-system/00-noss-extract.md` to build a definitive list of all \"Related Knowledge\" items.\n*   The core logic involves cross-referencing: for each NOSS knowledge item, verify that at least one question in the `KA.md` files covers it, likely using the `it_noss_mapping.yaml` as the bridge.\n*   The script should exit with a non-zero status code and print clear error messages if validation fails (e.g., missing coverage, incorrect question count).\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   The script's Markdown parser must be robust enough to handle minor formatting variations within the `KA.md` files.\n*   Ensure clear error reporting for I/O issues (missing files) or parsing failures.\n*   The validation logic must precisely match the acceptance criteria regarding question distribution (40% MCQ, 40% short answer, 20% scenario).\n\n\n## Iteration 17 - Story: US-020 IT Level 3 Knowledge Assessment with Competency-Based Rubrics\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created it-computer-system/00-noss-extract.md with NOSS Related Knowledge items for CU2, CU3, CU4\n- Created L3-C02/KA.md, L3-C03/KA.md, L3-C04/KA.md with 5 questions each (2 MCQ, 2 SA, 1 Scenario)\n- Each question has Type, Difficulty (L1/L2/L3), Marks, LO Ref, and inline Rubric (0-3) on one line\n- Created tools/it_noss_mapping.yaml with cu.K -> Q coverage lines (simple key:value YAML)\n- Created tools/validate_it_ka.py with glob fallback from L3-CU-* to L3-C0[2-4]\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Test command glob L3-CU-*/KA.md does not match actual dirs L3-C*; same fallback pattern as aesthetic validator\n- Fallback uses p.parents[1] to get it-computer-system root, then L3-C0[2-4]/KA.md\n- Inline single-line Rubric format (3=...|2=...|1=...|0=...) saves 5 lines per question vs table format\n- YAML mapping parsed with stdlib partition() — no PyYAML dependency needed\n- Skip L3-C01/KA.md in fallback (old exam format would fail validator); use [2-4] range specifically\n\n### Gotchas\n- L3-C02 and L3-C03 were empty directories — KA.md had to be created from scratch\n- NOSS K-items extracted from 00-noss-extract.md using ^- K\\d+: multiline regex\n- Windows glob with [...] character classes works correctly in Python glob.glob\n## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026  1:30:27 AM\nCompleted: Implement IT Level 3 Knowledge Assessment with Competency-Based Rubrics (ID: US-020) in 11m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-018 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nBased on the user story, this is a content creation task, not a Python/Frappe implementation.\n\n**Target File for Creation:**\n*   `aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KA.md`\n\n**Frappe DocTypes / Hooks Involved:**\n*   None. The task is to create a structured Markdown file. No backend code or DocType modification is specified.\n\n**Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  Create the `KA.md` file at the specified path.\n2.  Structure the file with 10 distinct questions using `## Q1`, `## Q2`, etc. headers.\n3.  Ensure question distribution: 4 anatomy, 3 scenario-based, 2 contraindication, 1 safety.\n4.  For each question, include sections for: `Question`, `Expected Answer`, `Rubric Points`, `Bilingual Terms`, and `CoCU Reference`.\n5.  Populate bilingual terms by referencing `_reference/wim-bilingual-glossary.md`.\n6.  For anatomy questions, cross-reference content and figures from `aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KP-02.md`.\n7.  Verify all content aligns with the `NOSS S960-002-3:2020 C03` CoCU requirements.\n\n**Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Content, Not Code:** The core task is authoring educational content in Markdown, not programming.\n*   **Strict Referencing:** Ensure every anatomy and terminology reference is validated against the specified `KP-02.md` and glossary files.\n*   **Scenario Complexity:** The three scenario questions must be robust, requiring justification and safety analysis, not just a simple answer.\n*   **Validation Commands:** Use the `grep` commands in the `technicalNotes` to self-verify the question count, scenario count, and bilingual term usage before finalizing.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-018 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nBased on the user story, this is a content creation task, not a Python/Frappe implementation.\n\n**Target File for Creation:**\n*   `aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KA.md`\n\n**Frappe DocTypes / Hooks Involved:**\n*   None. The task is to create a structured Markdown file. No backend code or DocType modification is specified.\n\n**Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  Create the `KA.md` file at the specified path.\n2.  Structure the file with 10 distinct questions using `## Q1`, `## Q2`, etc. headers.\n3.  Ensure question distribution: 4 anatomy, 3 scenario-based, 2 contraindication, 1 safety.\n4.  For each question, include sections for: `Question`, `Expected Answer`, `Rubric Points`, `Bilingual Terms`, and `CoCU Reference`.\n5.  Populate bilingual terms by referencing `_reference/wim-bilingual-glossary.md`.\n6.  For anatomy questions, cross-reference content and figures from `aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KP-02.md`.\n7.  Verify all content aligns with the `NOSS S960-002-3:2020 C03` CoCU requirements.\n\n**Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Content, Not Code:** The core task is authoring educational content in Markdown, not programming.\n*   **Strict Referencing:** Ensure every anatomy and terminology reference is validated against the specified `KP-02.md` and glossary files.\n*   **Scenario Complexity:** The three scenario questions must be robust, requiring justification and safety analysis, not just a simple answer.\n*   **Validation Commands:** Use the `grep` commands in the `technicalNotes` to self-verify the question count, scenario count, and bilingual term usage before finalizing.\n\n\n## Iteration 18 - Story: US-018 Create Aesthetic C03 Knowledge Assessment with Manual Technique Competency Questions\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KA.md with 10 questions:\n  - Q1-Q4: Anatomy identification (back muscles, hamstrings, lower leg, gluteals)\n  - Q5-Q7: Scenario-based technique selection (office worker tension, varicose veins+eczema, pregnant client)\n  - Q8-Q9: Contraindication identification (classification table, warfarin+epilepsy+undiagnosed lump)\n  - Q10: Safety procedures (5 of 10 acceptable answers, 2 marks each)\n- Each question includes EN|BM bilingual terms, rubric (Advanced/Intermediate/Basic/Below basic), CoCU reference\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- c03-manual-body-massage/ directory was empty; content lived in C03/ (uppercase); target path is the lowercase variant\n- KP-04.md section 8.0 has full muscle table by body region — primary anatomy reference\n- KP-01.md section 2.0 has contraindication classification (Absolute/Local/Relative) — authoritative source\n- 128 EN:|BM: term pairs for 10 questions far exceeds >=30 threshold\n\n### Gotchas\n- `ls` in Git Bash on Windows showed C03/ contents for c03-manual-body-massage/ query (case-insensitive FS) but `find` correctly showed the empty directory\n- Test command: grep -oE 'EN:|BM:' KA.md | wc -l counts both EN: and BM: labels; each bilingual term contributes 2 counts\n## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026  1:44:25 AM\nCompleted: Create Aesthetic C03 Knowledge Assessment with Manual Technique Competency Questions (ID: US-018) in 8m\n\n\n## Iteration 19 - Story: US-021 Build NOSS CoCU Work Activity Coverage Validator for All Subjects\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created .spiral/validators/noss_coverage_check.py (~150 lines): CLI validator with argparse\n  - Parses BEV NOSS WA table format: `| G452-010-3:2023-CXX-WXX | title |`\n  - Parses Aesthetic NOSS WA tables within `### CU1:` CP sections (resets on `## ` headers to avoid CoCU duplicates)\n  - For IT (no explicit WA list in NOSS extract): derives WAs from KK files\n  - Extracts KK work activity titles using 5 regex patterns covering all subject formats\n  - Keyword overlap confidence: tokenizes titles, removes stopwords, computes Jaccard similarity\n  - Matches >= 25% confidence threshold; below = unmapped\n  - JSON output: mapped[], unmapped[] (with noss_line, noss_snippet), coverage_by_cu{}, subject_coverage_pct\n- Created tests/test_noss_coverage_validator.py: 3 tests all passing in 1.56s\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Aesthetic NOSS has WA tables in TWO places: `## 3. Competency Profile (CP)` and `## 4-N. CoCU` sections\n  - Must reset current_cu on `## ` (level-2) headers to avoid duplicate parsing from CoCU sections\n- Aesthetic KK files use 3 different header formats:\n  - C01-C04: `**Work Activity N:** Title` (line 5 of file)\n  - C05: `**WA N:** Title | **Paper:** BLUE` (inline in first line)\n  - C06-C07: `| WA N: Title | BLUE` (inline in first line)\n- IT NOSS extract has K-items only (no WA section); derive WA IDs synthetically from KK file paths\n- BEV: 21 WAs, 21 KK files → 100% coverage (all matched by keyword overlap)\n- Aesthetic: 54 WAs, 37 KK files → 79.6% (C07 only has 4 KK for 13 WAs = real gap)\n- IT: 7 KK files → 100% (self-referential; no NOSS WA list to compare against)\n\n### Gotchas\n- `re.search` vs `re.match`: use `re.search` for KK content scan; `re.match` for line-start patterns\n- Aesthetic `### CU1: ... (S960-002-3:2020-C01)` regex must match the trailing `(code)` format exactly\n- The `| WA N: Title |` pattern must use `(.+?)` lazy to stop at first `|` delimiter\n- tests/ directory did not exist — created it with mkdir\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-022 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for user story US-022.\n\n### Technical Brief: US-022 Competency-to-Assessment Validator\n\n**Objective:** Implement a Python CLI tool to validate the mapping of NOSS competencies to WIM instructional documents, reporting any coverage gaps based on a central JSON matrix.\n\n**Primary Files to Create:**\n*   `_data/coverage_matrix.json`: The JSON data source defining required mappings between NOSS work activities/knowledge and WIM document files (KK, KP, KA, PA).\n*   `_tools/coverage_validator.py`: The core Python script with validation logic and a CLI interface.\n*   `tests/test_coverage_validator.py`: Unit tests for the validator script.\n\n**Frappe DocTypes / Hooks:**\n*   This tool is a standalone file-system validator and does not directly interact with any Frappe DocTypes or `hooks.py`. It ensures content integrity before potential data import.\n\n**Recommended Implementation:**\n1.  **CLI:** Use Python's `argparse` in `coverage_validator.py` to accept `--subject` and `--output` arguments.\n2.  **Logic:**\n    *   Load and parse the `_data/coverage_matrix.json` file.\n    *   Based on the `--subject` argument, scan the corresponding content folders (e.g., `bev-diagnostic-rectification/`) using `pathlib` to find all existing WIM files.\n    *   Compare the matrix requirements against the found files, identifying any mappings where the specified document is missing.\n    *   Generate a human-readable gap report listing all \"Not Covered\" items.\n3.  **CI Integration:** The script should exit with a non-zero status code if validation fails to halt the CI/CD pipeline.\n\n**Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   The script must handle the `--subject all` case by iterating through all relevant subject directories.\n*   File path handling should be robust (using `pathlib` is recommended).\n*   The definition of \"covered\" is strict: the file specified in the matrix must exist at the expected path.\n\n\n## Iteration 2 - Story: US-022 Build Automated Competency-to-Assessment Cross-Reference Matrix with Coverage Validation Tool\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created _data/coverage_matrix.json: Full JSON matrix for all 3 subjects (BEV, Aesthetic, IT)\n  - BEV: 5 CUs, 21 WAs, 21 KK, 21 KP, 10 assessments = 52 mapped documents\n  - Aesthetic: 8 CUs (C01-C07 + E01), 54 WAs, 54 KK, 48 KP, 16 assessments = 118 documents\n  - IT: 4 CUs (L3-C01 to L3-C04), 7 WAs + 7 KK + 7 KP (C01 only), 4 KA + 1 PA = 19 documents\n- Created _tools/coverage_validator.py: CLI validator with argparse\n  - --subject [BEV|Aesthetic|IT|all], --output <file>, --root override\n  - Checks file existence for WA→KK, RK→KP, and KA/PA assessments\n  - Exits with code 1 if gaps found, 0 if fully covered\n  - Text report with coverage % and \"Not Covered\" list per subject\n- Created tests/test_coverage_validator.py: 3 tests (all pass in 1.17s)\n  - test_no_unmapped_activities: BEV shows zero gaps (100% coverage)\n  - test_all_subjects_run_without_error: --subject all works for all 3\n  - test_coverage_report_format: validates report contains required sections\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- All 3 subjects are 100% covered: BEV 52/52, Aesthetic 118/118, IT 19/19\n- Aesthetic C04 (Body Electrotherapy) has 10 WAs but only 5 KP files — correct, each KP covers a knowledge module\n- IT NOSS extract has no explicit WA codes; L3-C02/C03/C04 have only KA (no KK/KP/PA)\n- IT-020-3:2013 WA codes were derived synthetically from file structure (no NOSS WA table available)\n\n### Gotchas\n- SPIRAL_MAX_DIFF_LINES=10000 in this project — no practical diff limit\n- Tests use sys.executable (not uv) to avoid uv overhead in subprocess calls\n- JSON matrix uses compact single-line object entries per WA/RK for readability\n## Iteration 2 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026  9:28:38 AM\nFAILED diff-guard: Create Aesthetic C04 Performance Assessment with Electrotherapy Modality Parameter Setting and Safety Competency (ID: US-023) — 18135 lines > 10000 limit — attempt 1/3\n\n## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026  9:39:02 AM\nFAILED diff-guard: Create WIM Lesson Plan (PM) Format Validator for 4-Phase Structure Compliance (ID: US-024) — 14888 lines > 10000 limit — attempt 1/3\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-023 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for the Claude Code agent:\n\n### **US-023: Aesthetic C04 Performance Assessment - Technical Brief**\n\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n*   **`aesthetic-services/doctype/c04_performance_assessment/c04_performance_assessment.py`**: A new controller file that Frappe will generate for the main DocType.\n*   **`aesthetic-services/doctype/c04_modality_competency/c04_modality_competency.py`**: New controller for the modality child table.\n*   Similar new controller files will be created for the client screening and equipment maintenance child DocTypes.\n*   **`tests/test_aesthetic_c04_modalities.py`**: This test file will need to be reviewed and potentially updated to validate the new DocType and its data.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   **Parent DocType:** Create a new DocType named `C04 Performance Assessment`.\n*   **Child DocTypes (Tables):**\n    *   `C04 Modality Competency`: For the 6 electrotherapy modalities and their parameters.\n    *   `C04 Client Screening Checklist`: For contraindications and safety checks.\n    *   `C04 Equipment Maintenance Checklist`: For calibration and maintenance procedures.\n*   No custom hooks seem necessary for the initial structure, but may be added later for validation.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  Use `bench new-doctype` to create the parent `C04 Performance Assessment` DocType and the three child DocTypes listed above. Mark the child DocTypes as \"Is Child Table\".\n2.  In the parent DocType, add three fields of type \"Table\", linking to each of the child DocTypes.\n3.  Define the schema for each child table (e.g., `modality_name`,