e-analysis for US-024 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for user story US-024:\n\n**Objective:** Create a Knowledge Assessment (KA) Markdown file for BEV C04, focusing on electric drive train diagnostics. This is a content generation task, not a Python coding task.\n\n**File to Create:**\n*   `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C04/KA.md`\n\n**Reference Source:**\n*   `bev-diagnostic-rectification/00-noss-extract.md`: Use this file to extract the specific `CoCU 4` assessment criteria required for the questions.\n\n**Implementation Approach:**\n1.  Create the new file `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C04/KA.md`.\n2.  Structure the document with `TAJUK`, `TUJUAN`, and `PENERANGAN` sections, adhering to the WIM format.\n3.  Author at least 8 scenario-based questions covering diagnostics for electric motors, inverters, and torque vectoring.\n4.  Incorporate calculation problems (e.g., efficiency loss, torque distribution).\n5.  For each question, create an answer key with a clear marking scheme.\n6.  Crucially, ensure each question's answer key explicitly references the corresponding `CoCU 4` assessment criteria from the NOSS source.\n\n**Validation & Gotchas:**\n*   The output file will be validated with `uv run python validators/coverage_validator.py bev-diagnostic-rectification/C04/KA.md --check-assessment-coverage`.\n*   Passing this validator is a key acceptance criterion.\n*   No Frappe DocTypes or Python application logic changes are required. The focus is entirely on the Markdown content's accuracy and format.\n\n\n## Iteration 4 - US-024: Create BEV C04 Knowledge Assessment with Electric Drive Train Diagnostics Scenarios\n\n### What was implemented\n- Rewrote `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C04/KA.md` (41 lines → 166 lines)\n- Added WIM TAJUK/TUJUAN/PENERANGAN header with bilingual EN/BM text\n- Q-Format structure: Bahagian A (Q1–Q20 MCQs with CoCU C04 refs), Bahagian B (Q21–Q28 scenario questions), Bahagian C (Q29–Q32 enrichment)\n- 8 scenario questions (Q21–Q28) covering inverter, traction motor, transaxle, brake control, safety\n- Calculation scenarios: Q26 (efficiency losses at 80% load), Q27 (torque distribution / AWD torque vectoring)\n- 36 CoCU C04 assessment criteria references across all questions\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- C02/KA.md is the \"golden\" example for BEV KA format — Q-Format with 40 questions, 25 marks total\n- CoCU references use format [CoCU C04.X.Y] where X=WA number, Y=work step number\n- The story test command path `validators/coverage_validator.py` is wrong — actual validator is `_tools/coverage_validator.py` with `--subject BEV` flag\n\n### Gotchas\n- The `--check-assessment-coverage` flag in the story test command doesn't exist; actual test is `uv run python _tools/coverage_validator.py --subject BEV`\n- Torque vectoring (Q27) satisfies \"torque distribution\" requirement — AWD split calculation covers the acceptance criterion\n## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026 10:14:55 AM\nCompleted: Create BEV C04 Knowledge Assessment with Electric Drive Train Diagnostics Scenarios (ID: US-024) in 11m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-025 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for the Claude agent:\n\n**Objective**: Implement story US-025 by creating a Performance Assessment (PA) markdown file for Aesthetic C01.\n\n**Project Context**: lhdn_payroll_integration (Frappe/Python), but this task focuses on structured content generation, not direct Frappe app modification.\n\n**Files to Modify/Create**:\n*   **Primary Target**: Create `aesthetic-services/C01/PA.md`.\n*   **Primary Reference**: Read `aesthetic-services/00-noss-extract.md` to get performance standards for the rubric.\n*   **Validation Script**: The output will be checked with `validators/assessment_validator.py`.\n\n**Frappe Context**:\n*   No direct DocType or `hooks.py` modifications are required. The task is to create a structured `.md` file that will likely be used by the Frappe system later.\n\n**Implementation Approach**:\n1.  Read the NOSS CoCU 1 performance standards from the reference file.\n2.  Create the `aesthetic-services/C01/PA.md` file.\n3.  Inside, develop 3 distinct client consultation scenarios (health history, skin analysis, recommendations).\n4.  For each scenario, create a 5-point scoring rubric as a markdown table.\n5.  The rubric's criteria must be derived from the NOSS standards and cover communication, clinical reasoning, and safety.\n6.  The overall file must follow the WIM PA structure, including `LANGKAH KERJA` and `SENARAI SEMAK` sections.\n\n**Gotchas & Edge Cases**:\n*   The validator (`assessment_validator.py`) enforces a strict structure. Pay close attention to the required markdown format for tables and headers.\n*   Ensure a clear and explicit link between the rubric criteria and the performance indicators found in the NOSS extract.\n*   The scenarios must be comprehensive enough to justify a 15-20 minute assessment duration.\n\n\n## Iteration 1 - US-025: Create Aesthetic C01 Performance Assessment with Client Consultation Scenario Rubrics\n\n### What was implemented\n- Rewrote `aesthetic-services/C01/PA.md` (164 lines → 153 lines)\n- 3 interactive consultation scenarios (15-20 min each):\n  - S1: Madam Rosnah — hypertension + shellfish allergy (health history screening)\n  - S2: Ms. Adilah — postpartum/breastfeeding (skin & body composition analysis)\n  - S3: Mr. Hafiz — BMI 30.2 + cellulite Grade 2-3 (treatment recommendation)\n- 5-point rubrics for 4 criteria per scenario: communication, health/allergy data, skin assessment, recommendations\n- Pass threshold: ≥3/5 per criterion, ≥12/20 per scenario, 2/3 scenarios to pass\n- SENARAI SEMAK safety compliance checklist retained\n- Coverage validator: 118/118 Aesthetic (100%)\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- `validators/assessment_validator.py` doesn't exist — actual test is `_tools/coverage_validator.py --subject Aesthetic`\n- Previous C01/PA.md had good structure (100 marks, safety checklist, 5 tasks) but lacked 3 separate scenarios with 5-point rubrics\n- 5-point rubric columns: 5-Cemerlang, 4-Memuaskan, 3-Kompeten, 2-Sedang Membangun, 1-Tidak Kompeten\n\n### Gotchas\n- Each scenario needs a different realistic client profile to justify independent 15-20 min assessment\n- Postpartum/breastfeeding is a key contraindication for electrotherapy — good discriminator for S2\n- Pass/fail language: \"≥12/20, no criterion <3\" makes both score AND minimum-per-criterion explicit\n## Iteration 2 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026 10:22:34 AM\nCompleted: Create Aesthetic C01 Performance Assessment with Client Consultation Scenario Rubrics (ID: US-025) in 5m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-026 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for user story US-026.\n\n**Subject: Technical Brief: US-026 WIM Markdown to DOCX Export Pipeline**\n\nThis brief outlines the implementation plan for creating a Python-based document conversion pipeline.\n\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n*   **Create:** `build/wim_md_to_docx.py`. This is the main script for the export logic.\n*   **Create/Update:** `tests/test_wim_export.py`. Add new test cases to validate the output `.docx` file's structure and styling, specifically checking the header content and format compliance as per the acceptance criteria.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   None. This user story describes a standalone build-time script for file conversion. It does not integrate with the Frappe framework and does not involve any DocTypes or hooks.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n*   Use `argparse` in `build/wim_md_to_docx.py` to handle CLI arguments: input file, output file, and `--style`.\n*   Use `pandoc` (called via `subprocess` or `pypandoc`) for the initial Markdown-to-DOCX conversion.\n*   Use the `python-docx` library to open and modify the generated DOCX.\n*   Load the `_reference/wim-docx-template.docx` to apply base styles (fonts, margins).\n*   Programmatically insert a header table/box on the first page and populate it by parsing the NOSS Code and CU from the input file path.\n*   Implement the `--style` flag to set the page background color based on the document type (e.g., KP, KT).\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **External Dependency:** The script requires `pandoc` to be installed and accessible in the system's PATH. This must be documented.\n*   **Styling Fidelity:** Replicating the precise JPK header and layout within DOCX can be complex. Extensive use of the `python-docx` API to manipulate tables, paragraphs, and sections will be necessary.\n*   **Metadata Parsing:** The logic to extract NOSS code and CU from file paths must be robust enough to handle variations.\n*   **Error Handling:** The script must gracefully handle file not found errors, invalid `--style` arguments, and failures from the `pandoc` subprocess.\n\n\n## Iteration 1 - US-026: Build WIM Markdown to DOCX Export Pipeline\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `build/wim_md_to_docx.py`: CLI with argparse (input, --style, --output), pandoc subprocess conversion, python-docx post-processing (JPK header box table, paper color watermark shading, 2.5cm margins)\n- Created `tests/test_wim_export.py`: 4 tests — header compliance, output creation, invalid style rejection, missing file error\n- Installed python-docx into .venv (uv venv + uv pip install)\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- pandoc-generated DOCX doesn't include \"Table Grid\" style — must add borders manually via w:tcBorders XML\n- Paper color watermark applied via w:shd paragraph shading (paragraph-level RGB fill)\n- NOSS code parsed from `**Kod WIM / WIM Code:**` frontmatter line via regex\n- CU parsed from path component matching C\\d+ pattern\n- Doc type inferred from file stem (KP-01 → KP)\n\n### Gotchas\n- `docx.util.Twips` doesn't exist in python-docx 1.2.0 — use `docx.shared` only\n- \"Table Grid\" style KeyError when using pandoc-generated DOCX as base — add borders manually\n- pytest must be installed in the same venv as python-docx for imports to work\n## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026 10:44:31 AM\nFAILED diff-guard: Create KT Question Diversity Analyzer with Question Type Distribution Metrics and Recommendations (ID: US-027) — 12453 lines > 10000 limit — attempt 1/3\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-030 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is a technical brief for implementing user story US-030.\n\n**Subject:** Technical Brief for US-030: Bloom's Taxonomy Question Classifier\n\n**1. Python Files for Modification:**\n*   **Primary Target (New File):** `_tools/classifiers/question_difficulty_classifier.py`. This script will contain the core ML-assisted logic for parsing, classification, and reporting.\n*   **Potential Integration Point:** `lhdn_payroll_integration/lhdn_payroll_integration/doctype/assessment/assessment.py`. A server script could be added here to trigger the classifier `on_submit` for a single assessment in real-time.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   **DocTypes:** The classifier will primarily read from `Assessment` and `Question` DocTypes (assuming these are the correct names for KA/PA assessments and their questions).\n*   **Hooks:** No direct `hooks.py` modifications are specified. The primary execution method is a standalone script, but a custom server script attached to the `Assessment` DocType is a logical extension.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  Inside `question_difficulty_classifier.py`, initialize the Frappe framework to use the ORM for database access.\n2.  Create a mapping of action verbs to Bloom's cognitive levels (1-6) (e.g., `{'define': 1, 'classify': 2, 'solve': 3}`).\n3.  Implement a `classify_question` function that takes question text, identifies the main action verb, and returns the corresponding Bloom's level and a confidence score.\n4.  Develop a main execution block that fetches all `Assessment` documents and their child `Question` documents.\n5.  For each assessment, iterate through its questions, classify each one, and aggregate the results to generate a cognitive distribution report (e.g., `{ \"Level 1\": \"25%\", \"Level 4\": \"15%\" }`).\n6.  Implement the flagging logic based on the acceptance criteria (<20% higher-order thinking or >50% at a single level).\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Verb Ambiguity:** A single verb can map to multiple cognitive levels depending on context. The initial version may need to default to the lowest possible level or use a simple keyword-based context check.\n*   **Execution Context:** The script is meant to be run via `uv run`. Ensure it correctly bootstraps the Frappe environment to access DocTypes and the database.\n*   **Scalability:** Processing all assessments across all subjects could be slow. The script should include logging to monitor performance and may require optimization or batch processing for large datasets.\n\n\n## Iteration N - Story: Create Assessment Question Difficulty Classifier with Bloom's Taxonomy Cognitive Level Tagging (US-030)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `_tools/classifiers/question_difficulty_classifier.py` — multi-format question parser + Bloom's verb classifier\n- Generated `_reference/assessment-difficulty-distribution.json` — compact per-assessment metrics (62 lines)\n- Handles 3 KA formats: BEV A1./B1./C1. style, Aesthetic Q1.(MCQ): style, IT **1.** plain numbered\n- Handles 4 PA patterns: TASK/SENARIO headings, numbered subtasks, bold rubric criteria, plain table rows\n- 27/40 assessments successfully parsed; all flagged as LOW_HOT (mean HOT 1.8%)\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- BEV KA uses `**A1.**`/`**B1.**`/`**C1.**` prefix with Section A/B/C structure\n- Aesthetic KA uses bilingual `**Q1 (MCQ):** **EN:**` format plus duplicate Section A/B/C\n- IT KA uses plain `**1.**` numbered with `- (a)` options (fallback pattern)\n- Tuinalogy files are in Chinese — English verb matching returns default L1 (acceptable)\n- PA files: BEV uses ## TASK headings with numbered subtasks; Aesthetic uses ### Task with plain table criteria; PA rubric uses `| **SX-A Title** |` bold format\n\n### Gotchas\n- JSON diff can easily exceed 350 lines if using `indent=2` for all assessments — use compact single-line per-assessment format\n- Multiple KA formats coexist: Q-style bilingual section + duplicate Section A/B/C in same file — use Q-style preferentially since it has type labels\n- PA plain table rows have false positives (header rows like \"Criteria\" or \"Task\") — filter by checking not in exclusion list\n- Script's `parents[2]` assumes `_tools/classifiers/` structure — verify ROOT is project root\n## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026 11:09:14 AM\nCompleted: Create Assessment Question Difficulty Classifier with Bloom's Taxonomy Cognitive Level Tagging (ID: US-030) in 15m\n\n\n## Iteration N - Story: Build WIM Header Box Template Enforcer for All Document Types Across All Subjects (US-031)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `_tools/enforcer/header_box_enforcer.py` — scans all WIM files (KP, KT, KK, KA, PA, PM) across 3 subjects (Aesthetic, BEV, IT-L3/L4/L5), validates 4 required header fields (code, effective, subject, level), reports non-compliance with per-doc-type template snippets, auto-inserts headers via --fix mode\n- Created `_reference/wim-header-template.md` — template snippets for all 6 doc types with NOSS codes table\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- WIM files in all subjects are missing `Effective`, `Subject`, `Level` fields — only have `Kod/Code` field currently\n- `--fix` mode inserts `2024-01-01` as default effective date (valid format, user must verify) with `<!-- verify date -->` comment\n- IT subject has L3/L4/L5 variants — each needs its own NOSS code entry in SUBJECTS dict\n\n### Gotchas\n- `--fix` modifies files in place — revert with `git checkout -- <subject>/` if test-running\n- `YYYY-MM-DD` placeholder in template doesn't pass `\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}` regex — must use real-format date in --fix mode\n- Unused `stem = path.stem.upper()` caused potential ruff warning — removed\n## Iteration 2 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026 11:18:08 AM\nCompleted: Build WIM Header Box Template Enforcer for All Document Types Across All Subjects (ID: US-031) in 7m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-032 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for implementing user story US-032.\n\n### **Technical Brief: US-032 - Assessment Question Type Analyzer**\n\n**1. Files to Modify/Create:**\n*   **Primary Target:** Create a new standalone Python script at `_tools/analyzers/question_type_analyzer.py`.\n*   **Input Files (Read-Only):** The script will need to read all Knowledge Assessment (KA) and Practical Assessment (PA) files. These are likely located within the subject-matter directories: `bev-diagnostic-rectification/`, `aesthetic-services/`, and `it-computer-system/`.\n*   **Output File:** The script will generate a Markdown report at `_reference/assessment-question-type-distribution.md`.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   None. This is a standalone Python analysis tool and does not interact with the Frappe framework, its DocTypes, or its hooks.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n*   Use Python's `argparse` library to handle the `--all-subjects` and `--format` command-line arguments.\n*   Implement a primary function to discover and glob all `KA` and `PA` assessment files from the subject directories.\n*   Create a \"QuestionClassifier\" class. Its main method will take file content and use regex patterns to identify and count question types (MCQ, short-answer, scenario, etc.) based on the structures defined in the acceptance criteria.\n*   Develop a \"DistributionCalculator\" to process the classified data, calculating the percentage of each question type per assessment and per subject.\n*   Add a \"RebalancingRecommender\" module that flags assessments meeting the low-diversity criteria (e.g., <4 types or >60% single type) and generates specific, actionable recommendations for converting question types.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Pattern Recognition:** The regex for identifying question types must be robust to avoid misclassifying questions. For example, \"blank lines\" for short-answer questions might be unreliable.\n*   **File Discovery:** Avoid hardcoding paths. The script should dynamically locate the subject directories to ensure it's adaptable.\n*   **Combined Questions:** Be aware of questions that might fit multiple types. Establish a clear heuristic for classification.\n*   **Error Handling:** The script must gracefully handle cases where no assessment files are found or a file cannot be parsed.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-032 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for implementing user story US-032.\n\n### **Technical Brief: US-032 - Assessment Question Type Analyzer**\n\n**1. Files to Modify/Create:**\n*   **Primary Target:** Create a new standalone Python script at `_tools/analyzers/question_type_analyzer.py`.\n*   **Input Files (Read-Only):** The script will need to read all Knowledge Assessment (KA) and Practical Assessment (PA) files. These are likely located within the subject-matter directories: `bev-diagnostic-rectification/`, `aesthetic-services/`, and `it-computer-system/`.\n*   **Output File:** The script will generate a Markdown report at `_reference/assessment-question-type-distribution.md`.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   None. This is a standalone Python analysis tool and does not interact with the Frappe framework, its DocTypes, or its hooks.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n*   Use Python's `argparse` library to handle the `--all-subjects` and `--format` command-line arguments.\n*   Implement a primary function to discover and glob all `KA` and `PA` assessment files from the subject directories.\n*   Create a \"QuestionClassifier\" class. Its main method will take file content and use regex patterns to identify and count question types (MCQ, short-answer, scenario, etc.) based on the structures defined in the acceptance criteria.\n*   Develop a \"DistributionCalculator\" to process the classified data, calculating the percentage of each question type per assessment and per subject.\n*   Add a \"RebalancingRecommender\" module that flags assessments meeting the low-diversity criteria (e.g., <4 types or >60% single type) and generates specific, actionable recommendations for converting question types.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Pattern Recognition:** The regex for identifying question types must be robust to avoid misclassifying questions. For example, \"blank lines\" for short-answer questions might be unreliable.\n*   **File Discovery:** Avoid hardcoding paths. The script should dynamically locate the subject directories to ensure it's adaptable.\n*   **Combined Questions:** Be aware of questions that might fit multiple types. Establish a clear heuristic for classification.\n*   **Error Handling:** The script must gracefully handle cases where no assessment files are found or a file cannot be parsed.\n\n\n## Iteration N - Story: Build Cross-Subject Assessment Question Type Distribution Analyzer with Rebalancing Recommendations (US-032)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `_tools/analyzers/question_type_analyzer.py` — detects 7 question types (MCQ, short_answer, scenario, practical, essay, matching, fill_in) across all KA/PA files in 3 subjects\n- Generated `_reference/assessment-question-type-distribution.md` — per-assessment and per-subject distribution tables with flagging and rebalancing recommendations\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- PA files always contain only `practical` type (100%) by design — PA is performance-based, not knowledge-based; flagging them for low diversity is expected\n- BEV KA MCQ uses `- A.` / `- B.` style options; Aesthetic uses inline `A. B. C. D.` on same line\n- IT KA uses `- (a)` style options; matched by fallback MCQ_Q_PAT\n\n### Gotchas\n- En-dash character (–) in regex string breaks `py_compile` on Windows (cp1252 encoding) — use plain ASCII dash only in regex strings\n- Always open files with `encoding='utf-8'` — standard Windows Python gotcha\n## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026 11:30:54 AM\nCompleted: Build Cross-Subject Assessment Question Type Distribution Analyzer with Rebalancing Recommendations (ID: US-032) in 4m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-033 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is a technical brief for the Claude Code agent.\n\n**Project:** US-033: NOSS CoCU Related Knowledge Dependency Mapper\n**Objective:** Create a standalone Python validation script to ensure every \"Related Knowledge\" (RK) item specified in `prd.json` is backed by a corresponding Knowledge Paper (KP) content file.\n\n**Files to Create:**\n*   `scripts/validate_rk_coverage.py`: The main executable script for the validation logic.\n*   `scripts/rk_parser.py`: A utility module to handle the parsing and extraction of RK data from `prd.json`.\n*   `tests/test_rk_dependency_mapper.py`: Unit and integration tests for the new validation tool.\n\n**Frappe Components Involved:**\n*   This task is a standalone script and does not directly interface with Frappe DocTypes or hooks. Its primary interaction is with the filesystem and the `prd.json` data file.\n\n**Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  The main script, `validate_rk_coverage.py`, should parse command-line arguments to accept the subjects for validation (e.g., `bev`, `aesthetic`, `it`).\n2.  It will read the `prd.json` file and use the `rk_parser.py` utility to extract all RK reference codes for the given subjects.\n3.  It should then scan the project directories to build a list of all existing KP files.\n4.  The core logic will compare the list of required RKs against the list of existing KP files.\n5.  Generate a console report detailing: (1) covered RKs, (2) orphaned RKs (defined in JSON but no file), and (3) undocumented KP files (file exists but not referenced in JSON).\n6.  For CI/CD integration, the script must exit with code 0 on success (100% coverage) or a non-zero code if there are any discrepancies, printing warnings to stderr.\n\n**Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   The mapping logic between an RK reference code and its expected KP filename must be precise and consistently applied.\n*   Implement JSON schema validation for the `RelatedKnowledge` structure within `prd.json` to handle malformed data gracefully.\n*   The file system scan for KP files should be efficient to avoid slow execution times. A single pass is recommended.\n\n\n\n## Iteration N - Story: Implement NOSS CoCU Related Knowledge Dependency Mapper (US-033)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `scripts/rk_parser.py` — parses NOSS extract .md files to extract RK items (BEV/Aesthetic: WA-scoped N.M format; IT: CU-scoped KN format)\n- Created `scripts/validate_rk_coverage.py` — scans KP files for RK section headings, builds covered/orphaned/undocumented report, exits 0 for full coverage, 1 for partial, 2 for invalid args\n- Created `tests/test_rk_dependency_mapper.py` — 13 tests, all passing\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- NOSS extract CU detection: use `**CU Code:** ...-C01` line (works for both BEV and Aesthetic formats)\n- BEV NOSS uses `#### WA N:` headings; Aesthetic uses `### WAN:` (no space before number)\n- IT NOSS extract only covers CU2/C03/C04 in K-format; IT KP files don't embed K-codes in headings → 0% IT coverage expected\n- KP file RK codes found via regex `^#{1,5}\\s+(\\d+\\.\\d+)\\b` on section headings\n- BEV current coverage: 61/153 (39%), Aesthetic: 46/224 (20%), IT: 0/15 (0%)\n\n### Gotchas\n- aesthetic `### WA1:` has no space between WA and number; BEV uses `#### WA 1:` with space — handle both with `WA\\s*(\\d+):`\n- IT KP files use `## 1.0`, `## 2.0` numbered sections not matching K-codes — IT will always show 0% until KP files are updated to use K-code headings\n## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026 11:55:33 AM\nFAILED diff-guard: Implement IT-020 Knowledge Assessment Completeness Auditor for All Levels (ID: US-034) — 3414 lines > 500 limit — attempt 1/3\n\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1419,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1419,"newLines":21,"lines":[" ## Iteration 1 - Fri, Apr 17, 2026 11:55:33 AM"," FAILED diff-guard: Implement IT-020 Knowledge Assessment Completeness Auditor for All Levels (ID: US-034) — 3414 lines > 500 limit — attempt 1/3"," ","+## Iteration 2 - Thu, Apr 17, 2026","+PASSED: US-034 - IT-020 KA Completeness Auditor (attempt 2, opus model)","+","+### What was implemented","+- scripts/audit_it_ka_completeness.py: Compact audit script (~150 lines) scanning L3/L4/L5","+- tests/test_it_ka_completeness_auditor.py: 7 tests covering matrix, JSON, field validation","+- Handles both KA formats: C01 uses `**N.**` questions, C02-C04 use `### QN` headings","+- Integration hook via check_prd_hook() scanning prd.json for IT CU references","+","+### Patterns discovered","+- NEVER use `ruff format` in this repo — it destroys files with NOT_YET_IMPLEMENTED stubs","+- L3-C01 KA has 22 questions (full exam format); C02-C04 have 5 each (compact format)","+- Keep scripts under 150 lines and tests under 80 lines to stay under 500-line diff limit","+","+### Gotchas","+- ruff format is broken (work-in-progress version), only use ruff check","+- Previous attempt was 3414 lines because it generated KA content; this attempt only audits","+"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
